WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.667
By the end of the module, you should be able to structure multi-step SQL with CTEs,

00:00:05.667 --> 00:00:12.617
trace a recursive query, choose the correct ranking function, analyze sequences with LAG and LEAD,

00:00:12.617 --> 00:00:16.782
and write explicit frames for running and moving calculations.

00:00:16.782 --> 00:00:21.887
You should also understand the difference between GROUP BY and PARTITION BY.

00:00:21.887 --> 00:00:24.857
The goal is not only to run the syntax.

00:00:24.857 --> 00:00:28.772
You should be able to explain the row meaning at every stage.

00:00:28.772 --> 00:00:33.847
Keep a real project in mind as we work through this section—for example,

00:00:33.847 --> 00:00:39.172
an operations team comparing each event with the previous and next event.

00:00:39.172 --> 00:00:46.302
The technical feature matters because it changes what questions we can answer reliably and how easily another

00:00:46.302 --> 00:00:48.082
analyst can understand our work.

00:00:48.082 --> 00:00:55.407
As you listen, try to connect each SQL or data concept to a decision you might need to

00:00:55.407 --> 00:00:58.917
defend later: what the data means, what one row represents, what can be missing,

00:00:58.917 --> 00:01:03.040
and what assumptions the transformation introduces.
